level up here because I think it's important to first separate educational technology from technologies that children use more broadly because not every use of technology is educational. So what I mean by that is a child, let's say, watching TikTok on their phone is certainly using a technology. But that is not the kind of technology that we're talking about in this report. We're talking about those digital products, those services that are being used or are being promoted as intended to be used to support learning. And then I'll actually like answer your question that within the ed tech space, there are important differences. You know, some tools are meant specifically for teaching. So if you think of like a tutoring platform or a digital reading program,

9
00:06:18.000 --> 00:07:12.000
those tools were designed with instruction in mind and with the outcome of that instruction being learning. Others, we would classify as having a broader purpose, but they're used to support learning. So think about like a video meeting platform or even, you know, an app that helps you design your own videos. Those weren't created for schools. They weren't created for instruction, but that doesn't mean that they aren't used by educators as part of the instructional experience. So I think a lot of times, you know, people will ask me, does ed tech work? And to me, that seems a little bit like asking, does transportation work? You know, we have bicycles, we have school buses, we have airplanes, and they all work to get people somewhere, but they all serve very different

