character. So the amount of time is that. the same, but the experience is not. And so when we're talking about educational technologies, whether they're in schools or whether you're using them at home to help supplement learning, the more useful place to start is, you know, what is the child actually doing and what that time is replacing? Those two questions tell us much more than the number of minutes alone. Now, I won't say that time doesn't matter, especially when technology is getting in the way or replacing things like sleep or relationships or other things that we know children need for healthy development. But you cannot, as a responsible parent or educator, treat every minute on a screen as if it has the same value. You need to look at the quality of that experience and the tradeoffs that it creates.

