00:15:54.000 --> 00:16:45.000
In general, ed tech is most useful when it offers a real advantage for the learning task. It should not be the default simply because a digital option exists. So, for example, technology can be super helpful when a student needs immediate feedback. Technology can be super helpful when they need practice at the right level or to even give them access to an experience like an AP course or access to a foreign language that would be hard to create in that particular classroom or a school that they're in. I could even think of like in a science class, a simulation that, you know, might let a student see a scientific process that's normally invisible or a practice tool that

20
00:16:45.000 --> 00:17:37.000
adjust as the student improves. But technology is a much weaker substitute for things like a rich discussion or a hands-on discovery or even encouragement from another person. So I would say that the rule of thumb that I generally use is use the technology when it adds something meaningful to the learning. For learning that depends on complex reasoning, when it depends on relationships, when it depends on working with other people, technology can certainly have a supporting role, but it shouldn't become the whole experience. And that balance is going to even look different when we talk about, you know, to complicate it more when we talk about, you know, different ages of children. We're going to take a short break. When we return, we'll talk about how effective

